{"causes":["The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter was created during a period of significant cultural and literary flourishing in Japan, marked by the rise of the Edo period and the influence of Chinese literature on Japanese storytelling."],"effects":["The story's exploration of environmentalism and the interconnectedness of human and natural worlds has inspired a new generation of eco-conscious readers and writers."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Saikaku","sourceName":"Saikaku","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","targetName":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","context":"As a prominent Japanese novelist, Saikaku created this influential work, which has since become a cornerstone of Japanese literature."},{"sourceSlug":"Edo period","sourceName":"Edo period","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","targetName":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","context":"During the Edo period, Japanese literature was heavily influenced by Chinese storytelling, leading to the development of distinct literary styles and themes, such as the monogatari."},{"sourceSlug":"Heavenly realm","sourceName":"Heavenly realm","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Kaguya","targetName":"Kaguya","context":"According to the narrative, Kaguya was sent to Earth from the heavenly realm, where she was raised by the bamboo cutter and his wife."},{"sourceSlug":"Disney","sourceName":"Disney","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","targetName":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","context":"In 2013, Disney produced an animated adaptation of The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter, introducing the story to a global audience."},{"sourceSlug":"University of Tokyo","sourceName":"University of Tokyo","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","targetName":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","context":"Researchers from the University of Tokyo conducted a linguistic analysis of the narrative, revealing a unique relationship between the story's language and the natural environment."},{"sourceSlug":"Environmentalism","sourceName":"Environmentalism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","targetName":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","context":"The story's exploration of environmentalism and the interconnectedness of human and natural worlds has inspired a new generation of eco-conscious readers and writers."},{"sourceSlug":"Chinese literature","sourceName":"Chinese literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","targetName":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","context":"The Edo period saw a significant influence of Chinese literature on Japanese storytelling, leading to the development of distinct literary styles and themes, such as the monogatari."}],"places":[{"name":"Edo","role":"Setting"},{"name":"Japan","role":"Country"},{"name":"Heavenly realm","role":"Setting"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-tale-of-the-bamboo-cutter"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q923593","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tale_of_the_Bamboo_Cutter"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter (Japanese: 竹取物語, Taketori Monogatari) is the earliest surviving Japanese prose narrative, composed c. 909–910 CE (with some scholars proposing 850–950 CE), written in Chinese characters (kanji) rather than in the kana that would later characterise Heian women's literature, and attributed to an unknown author. It is the founding text of Japanese narrative prose — referred to by Murasaki Shikibu in the Tale of Genji as 'the ancestor of all tales' (monogatari no oya) — and one of the earliest examples of science fiction in world literature.

The tale narrates the discovery by an old bamboo cutter (Taketori no Okina) of a tiny luminous girl inside a shining bamboo stalk, whom he raises as his daughter, naming her Kaguya-hime ('Shining Princess'). As she grows, her extraordinary beauty attracts five noble suitors (including the Emperor himself), each of whom she sends on impossible quests for legendary treasures — a robe of fire-rat fur, the jewelled branch of the island of Hōrai, the stone begging bowl of the Buddha, the jewel from a dragon's neck, and the cowrie-shell born from a swallow — all of which fail. When the suitors (and the Emperor) are all rebuffed, Kaguya-hime reveals that she is from the Moon — the armies of Heaven descend to return her, and she ascends to the Moon, leaving the grieving Emperor a letter and the elixir of immortality, which he orders burned on the summit of Mount Fuji, the 'mountain nearest Heaven'.

The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter is the founding text of Japanese mono no aware aesthetics — the beauty of transience and loss — and the source of Japan's most enduring cultural image: the Moon as the origin of celestial beings and the destination of the soul. The story is still actively told and retold in manga (Princess Kaguya), anime (Isao Takahata's The Tale of Princess Kaguya, 2013), and pop culture worldwide.","subjects":["Japanese Literature","Ancient Era","Heian Period","Monogatari","Science Fiction Origins","Folk Tale","Kaguya-hime","Japanese Mythology"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter has had a lasting impact on Japanese literature and culture, influencing works such as Disney's Princess Kaguya and inspiring adaptations in theater, film, and opera.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
